# Rule-trigger items: the harness prefixes "First read the whole file <read> with the Read tool. Then answer: ...". After the restructure each `read` should load the matching .claude/rules/<rule>.md; before it, the answer comes from CLAUDE.md. Graded like recall.
{"id": "rule-faces-separation", "rule": "faces", "read": "photosearch/face_verify.py", "prompt": "In verify-face-labels, what is separation(P, Q), and why is it a percentile rather than min()?", "any": [["5th\\W+percentile", "\\bp5\\b", "SEPARATION_PERCENTILE", "percentile\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}5\\b", "\\b5\\W+percentile"], ["(one|a single|a few|single|few)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}(bad|wrong|mis)\\w*\\W*(label|face)", "\\bdrag", "disabl", "outlier", "\\b(zero|0)\\b"]], "reject": ["min\\(\\)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(is\\W+)?(too\\W+)?(slow|expensive)", "for\\W+speed"], "facts": "separation(P, Q) is a low percentile (the 5th percentile, SEPARATION_PERCENTILE = 5) of the distances between the persons' face sets, not min(). One bad label would drag a min() to zero and silently disable the pair; the percentile tolerates a few bad labels.", "counter": ["separation is the 50th percentile (median) of the pairwise distances; a percentile is used because min() is slow on large sets.", "It is the mean distance between P's and Q's centroids; min() is not robust to noise."], "pass_example": "`separation(P, Q)` is the 5th percentile of pairwise L2 distances between person P's reference faces and person Q's reference faces (lines 187, `SEPARATION_PERCENTILE = 5.0`). It uses a percentile instead of `min()` because a single mislabelled reference face would collapse `min()` to near zero, silently disabling calibration for that pair — \"no conflicts found\" is the most dangerous failure mode (lines 27-30). A low percentile is robust to a few bad labels while still capturing how close two people genuinely get (lines 181-182)."}
{"id": "rule-llm-unusable-answer", "rule": "llm-passes", "read": "photosearch/describe.py", "prompt": "What does the worker do when describe.UnusableAnswer is raised for a photo, and how does that differ from a transport timeout?", "any": [["failure\\W+row", "\\bfailures\\b", "FailedResult", "index_errors", "(one|an)\\W+attempt", "attempt\\W+(is\\W+)?spent", "spends?\\W+(\\w+\\W+)?attempt"], ["defer", "omit", "(no|not|without|doesn't|does not)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}(spend|burn|cost|consum)\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}attempt", "retr(y|ied)\\W+later"]], "reject": ["both\\s+(are|get)\\s+(treated\\s+the\\s+same|deferred|transient)", "UnusableAnswer\\W+(is\\W+)?(also\\W+)?(treated\\W+as\\W+)?(transient|deferred)\\b"], "facts": "describe.UnusableAnswer is a bad-output failure: the worker reports it as a failure row (FailedResult in the `failures` list), which spends one attempt and logs an index_errors row, nothing written. A transport timeout is transient: it is omitted/deferred and does not spend an attempt.", "counter": ["Both are deferred: UnusableAnswer is transient like a timeout, so there is no failure row and no attempt spent.", "UnusableAnswer is retried immediately in-process three times; a timeout aborts the batch."], "pass_example": "When `describe.UnusableAnswer` is raised for a photo, the worker records a **failure row**, spending one of that photo's `MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS` attempts and logging an entry to `index_errors`. The photo is eventually retired when it exhausts its attempt budget.\n\nA **transport timeout** (TimeoutError/ConnectionError) is the opposite: the worker defers the photo for retry without spending an attempt, so it can be re-claimed indefinitely.\n\n**Key distinction** (lines 268–281): UnusableAnswer is \"Deliberately NOT a TimeoutError / ConnectionError: it is the PHOTO's attempt, not the worker's transport problem.\" The model answered, but the answer was bad (cut off, refusal, garbage output) — that's the photo's fault, not a network stall. Nothing is written to the database either way, but an UnusableAnswer spends the attempt cap while a transport error defers the photo."}
{"id": "rule-ingest-faces-done", "rule": "ingest", "read": "photosearch/batch_state.py", "prompt": "Which worker pass counts an exhausted photo with no output toward `done` instead of `failed`, and why is that only safe for that pass?", "expect": ["\\bfaces\\b"], "any": [["nobody", "no\\W+faces?\\b", "no\\W+detectable", "empty\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(result|output|list)", "faces:\\s*\\[\\]"]], "reject": ["faces\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(counts?|is\\W+counted|goes)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(failed|toward\\W+failed)"], "counter": ["describe: an exhausted photo with no output counts as done since an empty description is fine; the faces pass counts it as failed (nobody may be in it).", "The clip pass, because an empty embedding is valid."], "pass_example": "**`faces`** is the only worker pass that counts an exhausted photo with no output toward `done` instead of `failed` (line 220, `_EMPTY_OUTPUT_IS_DONE`).\n\n**Why it's safe only for `faces`:** A photo with nobody facing the camera legitimately produces zero face rows — that's not a failure, it's a valid result. The pass records such photos with `terminal=True` on the first clean empty detection so they're detected once rather than three times (lines 208–210). In contrast, other passes like `describe` or `category-content` expect output on every eligible photo: no description or tags *is* a failure. On the first real batch (2026-09-19), 113 photos had no detectable faces; counting them as `failed` incorrectly blocked dependent steps (`match_faces`, `warm_crops`, `rank_measure`) behind a finished pass (lines 212–217). The pass's `detail` field reports the count, so no failure is hidden."}
{"id": "rule-maintenance-stacking-incremental", "rule": "maintenance", "read": "photosearch/maintenance.py", "prompt": "Is the maintenance sweep's stacking stage a full re-detect or missing-only? What ledger makes that possible and what can it never do?", "expect": ["stacking_seen"], "any": [["(?<!not )(?<!isn't )(?<!not fully )incremental", "missing-only", "dirty", "run_incremental_stacking"], ["never\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,6}(wipe|clear)", "(never|not|cannot|can't|must\\W+not)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}clear_stacks", "unscoped", "photo_ids\\s*=\\s*\\[\\]"]], "reject": ["(?<!not )(?<!not a )(?<!never a )(?<!never )full\\W+re-?detect\\W+(each|every)\\W+(night|run|sweep)", "stacking_seen\\W+(is\\W+)?(unused|not\\W+used)", "(?<!never )(?<!not )\\bcalls\\W+clear_stacks"], "facts": "The sweep's stacking stage is missing-only (incremental), via stacking.run_incremental_stacking and the stacking_seen ledger (only dirty photos are re-evaluated; it writes a diff). It must never call clear_stacks() or an unscoped detect_stacks/run_stacking, and never pass photo_ids=[] (read as the whole library).", "counter": ["It's a full re-detect every night (not incremental); stacking_seen is unused, and it calls clear_stacks first. It can never be scoped.", "Missing-only, via a dirty flag on photos; it can never run on videos."], "pass_example": "**Missing-only.** The `_stage_stacking()` function (line 403) calls `stacking.run_incremental_stacking()` with the `stacking_seen` ledger, which tracks photos already considered for stacking. The docstring says \"Missing-only burst/bracket stack detection\" — it detects new, re-timed, or altered-membership stacks only; unchanged stacks keep their id and hand-set top. The old behavior was a full-library re-detect that cleared every stack nightly (reported `would 157815, applied 27165` with zero changes).\n\n**What it can never do:** Call `db.clear_stacks()`, pass an unscoped `detect_stacks()`, or pass `photo_ids=[]` (which `detect_stacks` reads as the whole library). Write the ledger last so an abort leaves it dirty for the next run."}
{"id": "rule-workers-failures-list", "rule": "workers", "read": "photosearch/worker.py", "prompt": "When processing a photo raises a non-transient exception in a worker pass, what does the worker send to the server for it, and what does the server do with that?", "any": [["\\bfailures\\b", "FailedResult", "failure\\W+row", "_failure_row"], ["(spend|spent|spends|burn|cost|consum|increment|count|uses?)\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,5}\\w*attempt", "attempts?\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(increment|\\+\\s*1|spent|counted)", "index_errors"]], "reject": ["(?<!older )(?<!old )\\bserver\\W+(just\\W+|simply\\W+)?ignores\\b", "nothing\\W+is\\W+(logged|recorded)"], "counter": ["It sends a failures entry, which the server ignores: no attempt is spent and nothing is logged.", "It sends nothing; the server notices the missing result and retries the photo."], "pass_example": "Looking at the worker code:\n\n**What the worker sends:** When a non-transient exception occurs during photo processing, the worker calls `_failure_row()` (line 502), which creates a dict `{\"photo_id\": photo_id, \"error\": error_message}` (capped at 500 chars). These failure rows are separated from successful results via `_submit_kwargs()` (line 519) into a dedicated `failures` list and POSTed to `/api/worker/submit-results`.\n\n**What the server does:** According to the docstring at line 503-507, the server:\n1. **Spends one of the photo's `MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS`** (3, defined in `db.py`) on it\n2. **Writes nothing to result columns** — only records the failure\n3. **Lets `MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS` cap retire it** — once the photo hits 3 failed attempts, the `mark_processed(..., attempts >= MAX_PROCESS_ATTEMPTS)` predicate excludes it from future claims forever\n\nThis contract (illustrated in `.claude/rules/workers.md` line 14-16) prevents poison photos from being re-claimed indefinitely."}
{"id": "rule-video-probe-owner", "rule": "video", "read": "photosearch/video_probe.py", "prompt": "Which machine runs the video probe and scan, and what is the NAS's role for video?", "any": [["(worker|desktop|native)\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,6}(run|do|does|own|probe|scan)", "(probe|scan)\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,6}(worker|desktop|native)"], ["never\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}(decode|probe)", "(only\\W+)?(stores|serves)", "Range", "walks"]], "reject": ["NAS\\W+(itself\\W+|also\\W+|then\\W+)?(runs|probes|decodes|calls|does)\\W+(the\\W+)?(video\\W+)?(probe|scan|ffprobe|ffmpeg)"], "counter": ["The NAS runs the probe and scan nightly; the workers only describe frames, and the NAS serves the results.", "Any machine; the NAS decodes thumbnails for video."], "pass_example": "**Desktop (D19) runs video probe and scan.** Lines 4–6: \"Runs on the DESKTOP (D19) against the NAS's `/api/photos/{id}/full?purpose=worker` URL, which is Range-capable and rate-capped\" — three HTTP Range reads per video (magic check, ffprobe, cv2 first frame).\n\n**NAS role:** Serves Range-capable reads via `/api/photos/{id}/full?purpose=worker`, stores uploads (posters/crops via `POST /api/videos/{id}/assets`), runs Live-pairing SQL at probe submit, inserts rows, and unwraps ZIP-wrapped videos. **The NAS never decodes or runs ffprobe/ffmpeg** (line 485, rules/video.md)."}
{"id": "rule-print-topaz-exit-code", "rule": "print", "read": "photosearch/upscale.py", "prompt": "How does the Topaz upscale integration decide that a run succeeded, and why not the exit code?", "any": [["output\\W+file\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}(appear|exist|present|created|written)", "(appear|exist|present|created|written)\\w*\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}output", "new\\W+file", "file\\W+shows\\W+up"], ["\\b9\\b", "-1\\b", "127", "(never|not|isn't)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,2}(0|zero)\\b", "non-?zero", "unreliable|lies|meaningless|wrong"]], "reject": ["(checks?|requires?|tests?)\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}exit\\W+code\\W+(is\\W+|==\\W*|of\\W+)?0\\b", "output\\W+file\\W+(is\\W+)?ignored"], "facts": "Success is decided by an output file having appeared, not by the exit code: a good Topaz run exits 9 under WSL interop (never 0), and the broken tpai.exe exits -1 and writes nothing. So never judge by exit code and never use check=True.", "counter": ["It checks that the exit code is 0 (check=True); the output file is ignored.", "It parses Topaz's stdout for 'Done'; the exit code is fine but slow to arrive."], "pass_example": "`run_topaz()` (line 296) succeeds by **output file appearance**, not exit code. Before running Topaz, it snapshots existing files in the output directory (line 329), then checks whether new files appeared afterward (line 374). If none appeared, it raises `TopazError` regardless of exit code; if any appeared, it returns the newest file's path (line 380).\n\nWhy not exit code? A successful run exits **9** under WSL interop or **127** under Git Bash—never 0 (documented in module docstring, lines 29-33). Only the documented failure codes in `_ERROR_CODES` {255, 254, 253, 1} are reliable (line 370). The logic checks: if `returncode in _ERROR_CODES and returncode not in _SUCCESS_CODES` (line 370), raise; otherwise require `produced != []` (line 375). This avoids adding `check=True` to subprocess.run, which would crash every successful run."}
{"id": "rule-frontend-ps-poll", "rule": "frontend", "read": "frontend/dist/shared.js", "prompt": "What does PS.poll do differently from setInterval, and what incident motivated it?", "expect": ["setInterval"], "any": [["settle", "after\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,5}(resolve|settle|return|finish|complete)", "previous\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,3}(request|call|poll|fetch|one|promise)", "re-?arm", "in-flight"], ["SMB", "2026-09-19", "40\\W+(request\\W+|worker\\W+|server\\W+)?threads", "starv", "(thread|request)s?\\W+(?:\\w+\\W+){0,4}(pile|stack|fill|exhaust)", "wedg"]], "facts": "PS.poll re-arms only after the previous call's promise settles (and pauses while the tab is hidden), whereas setInterval fires regardless of whether the last request returned. Motivated by an incident where setInterval polling on a starved NAS (SMB/disk) stacked requests and filled all ~40 request threads, wedging the status page and the box it reported on.", "counter": ["PS.poll is just setInterval with a shorter default interval and pauses while hidden; motivated by a 40% CPU spike in the browser.", "PS.poll re-arms after the previous call settles, unlike setInterval; motivated by a memory leak in Chrome."], "pass_example": "**PS.poll vs. setInterval:**\n\n`PS.poll` (line 3283) re-arms only **after** `fn`'s promise settles, whereas `setInterval` fires on schedule regardless. It also pauses when the tab is hidden, returns a `stop()` function to abort in-flight calls, and requires `fn` to **return** its fetch chain.\n\n**Incident:** On **2026-09-19**, the NAS's disks saturated; each status poll took minutes. Two open tabs using `setInterval` kept issuing more requests until all 40 request threads were stuck, wedging the status page itself. `PS.poll` prevents this—a slow server is polled **slower**, never harder: at most one call is in flight per poller."}
{"id": "rule-search-compose-scope", "rule": "search", "read": "photosearch/search.py", "prompt": "What does _compose_scope in search_combined do, and which three filter kinds does it fold into the id scope?", "expect": ["scope", "\\bdates?\\b|date_from", "camera", "people|person"], "counter": ["_compose_scope merges the CLIP text scope with the tag and color filters into one id list.", "It builds the ORDER BY for date, location and rating."], "pass_example": "Based on the code, `_compose_scope` (lines 1589–1660) composes **index-backed structured filters into a single id scope** for optimization. When two or more such filters are active, it runs them together in one SQL query instead of separately, then every other filter runs only over those ids — reducing I/O dramatically (e.g., 1.9 GB cold down to a narrower set).\n\nThe **three filter kinds** it folds into the id scope are:\n\n1. **Date range** (`date_from`/`date_to`, lines 1633–1635)\n2. **Camera model** (`camera`, lines 1630–1632)  \n3. **People/persons** (`person_ids`, lines 1636, 1644–1651)\n\nWhen none exist, it returns `None` (line 1606); when the result exceeds `_SCOPE_MAX_IDS` (20k ids), it's dropped and filters run unscoped (line 1660). Video-specific filters (`videos_only`, `subject_label`) are also handled but are secondary to these three core structured filters (as documented in `.claude/rules/search.md`)."}
